Evidence Note 17/30 — EUDR

Geolocation is the data point most Brazilian files have — and almost none have in usable form.

The typical situation:
• the cooperative has coordinates, in a WhatsApp message;
• the CAR (Rural Environmental Registry) has a polygon, for the property, not the plot;
• the exporter has a list of supplier names, no coordinates;
• the buyer needs plot-level geolocation tied to each export lot, in a format the EU information system accepts.

Four datasets, one requirement, zero reconciliation.

What "usable" means here:
1. one geolocation per plot of production (not per farm, not per cooperative);
2. polygon where the plot exceeds the regulatory threshold, point otherwise;
3. a link from each plot to the production period and to the lot shipped;
4. a record of who supplied the coordinates and when.

Fact: the EUDR requires geolocation of all plots of land where the relevant commodities were produced.
Interpretation: the CAR is a useful source, not a substitute — it answers a Brazilian question, not the EU operator's.

The work is reconciliation, not surveying. Most of the data exists. It has not been made into evidence.

